#
# NEXTBSD-MACMINI -- Apple Mac mini M4 (Mac16,10, board J773gAP, SoC t8132).
#
# COVERAGE: this has never been booted. Nothing here has run on the hardware.
# It is a config that expresses the machine and compiles; that is all it is,
# and on this platform a config that compiles is worth roughly one boot cycle
# of confidence and no more. Read the serial log; do not assume.
#
# Unlike the Pi boards, the console is not the hard part here. Apple's own
# iBoot drives this UART and its Stage 1 and Stage 2 output can be captured
# from a second Mac over USB-C before any of our code runs -- so the expected
# output is observable in advance rather than guessed at. See the
# apple-silicon-bringup skill.
#
# The hard part is being allowed to boot at all: iBoot only transfers control
# to an object whose hash is in a Secure Enclave-signed LocalPolicy, and
# changing that policy needs a human in 1TR. That cost is paid once. After
# m1n1 stage 1 is installed, kernels push over USB with no reboot and no disk
# write.
#
include NEXTBSD

ident		NEXTBSD-MACMINI

# t8132. Gates arm64/apple/{exynos_uart,apple_pinctrl,apple_wdog,apple_aic}.c
# via patches/0030.
#
# Read from Asahi's t8132.dtsi, which is the same blob m1n1 hands us:
#
#   serial@3ad200000  "apple,s5l-uart"                             matches
#   wdt               "apple,t8132-wdt", "apple,t8103-wdt"         matches (fallback)
#   pinctrl           "apple,t8132-pinctrl", "apple,t8103-pinctrl" matches (fallback)
#   aic@381180000     "apple,t8132-aic3", "apple,t8122-aic3"       does NOT match
#
# Apple declares t8132 compatible with the t8103 programming model for the
# watchdog and pinctrl by listing t8103 as a fallback compatible, so those
# attach unchanged. The AIC deliberately does not: t8132 is AICv3 and
# apple_aic.c matches only "apple,aic". It declines to attach rather than
# attaching to hardware it does not describe -- the safe failure, and the
# opposite of what the BCM2712 drivers did on the Pi 500+.
#
# So this kernel currently has no interrupt controller. That is expected and
# is the next milestone, not a bug in this file.
options 	SOC_APPLE_T8132

# Early console. UART0 at 0x3ad200000, confirmed from the device tree
# (serial@3ad200000, reg = <0x3 0xad200000 0x0 0x1000>) and audible in iBoot's
# own Stage 2 output on the same lines.
#
# locore.S maps one 2MB device block at SOCDEV_PA and keeps the offset within
# it, so the exact register address is the right value to give here.
#
# 0x3ad200000 needs a MOVZ/MOVK pair (0xad20 << 16 | 0x3 << 32), so it depends
# on patches/0014, which replaced `mov xN, #imm` with `ldr xN, =expr` in
# locore.S for exactly this reason on the Pi's 0x107d001000. Without it this
# does not assemble.
options 	SOCDEV_PA=0x3AD200000
options 	EARLY_PRINTF=s5l

# A real console for the whole boot, not just the early window.
#
# EARLY_PRINTF above covers printf only up to cninit(), which drops early_putc
# unconditionally -- a boot that prints and then goes silent at exactly that
# point is this, not a hang. See config/macmini.env for why each tag in it is
# required, and in particular why rs:0 here where the Pi needs rs:2.
#
# This depends on patches/0031. hw.uart.console's dt: tag is resolved by
# uart_getclass() walking the uart_class_set linker set, and exynos_uart.c did
# not register its classes there -- so before that patch `dt:s5l` fell through
# and uart_cpu_getdev() handed the S5L registers to uart_ns8250_class, which
# puts garbage on the wire rather than raising an error. See #117.
env		"macmini.env"

# The FDT route may also work on its own: m1n1 rewrites /chosen at runtime and
# uart_cpu_fdt.c resolves stdout-path through the compat table rather than the
# linker set. That is worth reading off the wire on the first boot that reaches
# cninit(), but it is not something to depend on sight unseen.
